---

<Layout
  title="How to Change Image Width and Height | Image Size Changer"
  description="Learn how to accurately change digital image width and height in pixels or percentage. Discover aspect ratio formulas and practical scaling tips."
  structuredData={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema]}
>
  <Header />

  <main class="legal-page">
    <div class="container narrow-container">

      <div class="status-pill">
        <span class="status-dot"></span>
        Guide · Dimension Editing
      </div>

      <h1>How to Change Image Width and Height</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        Adjusting the width and height of an image is one of the most common tasks in digital design, photography, and web management. Whether you need an exact pixel count for a website banner or a percentage reduction for email, understanding how dimensions work ensures your photos remain sharp and undistorted.
      </p>

      <div class="legal-card">
        <h2>What Are Digital Image Dimensions?</h2>
        <p>
          Every digital raster image (such as JPG, PNG, or WebP) is formed by a rectangular grid of microscopic color squares known as <strong>pixels (px)</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Width:</strong> The total number of horizontal pixel columns across the image.</li>
          <li><strong>Height:</strong> The total number of vertical pixel rows down the image.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          When you see an image described as <code>1920 × 1080 px</code>, it means the graphic is 1,920 pixels wide and 1,080 pixels high, totaling 2,073,600 individual pixels.
        </p>

        <h2>Pixels vs. Percentage Scaling</h2>
        <p>
          When resizing with an online tool like <a href="/resize-image">ImageSizeChanger</a>, you can typically scale using two different methods:
        </p>

        <h3>1. Exact Pixel Scaling (px)</h3>
        <p>
          Pixel scaling lets you enter an exact target width and height. This method is essential when:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A website layout requires a container width of exactly <code>800 px</code> or <code>1200 px</code>.</li>
          <li>An application portal specifies maximum dimensions such as <code>600 × 600 px</code>.</li>
          <li>You are creating a social media graphic matching exact platform guidelines (e.g. <code>1080 × 1080 px</code>).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>2. Percentage Scaling (%)</h3>
        <p>
          Percentage scaling allows you to scale an image relative to its original size without calculating pixel math. For example:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Setting dimensions to <strong>50%</strong> halves both the width and height, reducing total pixels by 75%.</li>
          <li>Setting dimensions to <strong>25%</strong> reduces a giant 4000px camera photo down to a lightweight 1000px graphic suitable for quick messaging.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Golden Rule: Preserve Aspect Ratio</h2>
        <p>
          The most critical concept when changing dimensions is the <strong>aspect ratio</strong>—the proportional relationship between width and height.
        </p>
        <p style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid #9cff00; border-radius: 8px;">
          <code>Aspect Ratio = Original Width ÷ Original Height</code>
        </p>
        <p>
          If you change the width of an image from <code>2000 px</code> down to <code>1000 px</code> without adjusting height, the image becomes squashed. To prevent distortion, always keep the <strong>Aspect Ratio Lock</strong> enabled. When locked:
        </p>
        <p style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid #9cff00; border-radius: 8px;">
          <code>New Height = Math.round(New Width ÷ (Original Width ÷ Original Height))</code>
        </p>

        <h2>Common Standard Dimension Reference</h2>
        <div class="table-container">
          <table class="guide-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Use Case</th>
                <th>Standard Width (px)</th>
                <th>Standard Height (px)</th>
                <th>Aspect Ratio</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Square Social Post (Instagram)</td>
                <td>1080</td>
                <td>1080</td>
                <td>1:1</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Social Feed Link Preview (Facebook, LinkedIn)</td>
                <td>1200</td>
                <td>630</td>
                <td>1.91:1</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Full HD Desktop Display / YouTube Video</td>
                <td>1920</td>
                <td>1080</td>
                <td>16:9</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Standard Blog Content Column Image</td>
                <td>1200</td>
                <td>675</td>
                <td>16:9</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Website Thumbnail / Avatar</td>
                <td>300</td>
                <td>300</td>
                <td>1:1</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Step-by-Step: Changing Dimensions on ImageSizeChanger</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Open the <a href="/resize-image">Online Image Resizer</a>.</li>
          <li>Drag and drop your JPG, PNG, or WebP photo into the upload workspace.</li>
          <li>Verify your original width and height displayed in the status bar.</li>
          <li>Choose your unit: <strong>Pixels</strong> or <strong>Percent</strong>.</li>
          <li>Ensure the aspect ratio lock (🔗) is highlighted blue/active.</li>
          <li>Type your target width or height. The complementary dimension will calculate automatically.</li>
          <li>Select your preferred output format and click <strong>Resize Image</strong> to download.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <h3>Does changing dimensions change file size?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. Downscaling dimensions reduces the total number of pixels that must be encoded, which significantly reduces the file size in Kilobytes (KB) or Megabytes (MB).
        </p>

        <h3>Can I change width without changing height?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes, by unlocking the aspect ratio lock. However, doing so will stretch or squash the subject matter. If you need a specific rectangular ratio without stretching, crop the image rather than stretching it.
        </p>

        <h2>Related Guides</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/guides/image-aspect-ratio-guide/">Understanding Image Aspect Ratio</a></li>
          <li><a href="/guides/image-size-vs-file-size/">Image Size vs File Size: What's the Difference?</a></li>
          <li><a href="/guides/how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality/">How to Resize an Image Without Losing Quality</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem; display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <a class="back-link" href="/guides">← Back to all guides</a>
        <a class="back-link" href="/resize-image">Open Image Resizer →</a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
